WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.720
I'm in Helsingborg, in Sweden, to film a museum.

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.840
And normally I don't travel just to film museums,

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.860
because most museums -- science, natural history --

00:00:06.860 --> 00:00:08.900
are pretty much the same the world over.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.460
But this museum is not only interesting,
it's also unique.

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:14.780
Welcome to the Museum of Failure.

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:18.060
80-90% of all innovation projects fail.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.220
Everybody in the business knows this.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.280
And where are these failures?

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:25.080
Companies sweep them under the carpet
and pretend they don't exist.

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:28.960
The Museum of Failure takes these failed products,
puts them on display,

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:30.880
so that everybody can learn from them.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.280
The criteria that a product
has to meet to be here is:

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.400
that it was an innovation,
and that it was a failure.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.040
and the failure is a deviation
from expected, desired outcomes.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.560
(I didn't know Apple even had a gaming console!)

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:44.240
No, this is their hidden, dark secret(!)

00:00:44.260 --> 00:00:48.180
This one was supposed to measure
the EEG of a dog's brain

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:51.740
and then translate those mental states
into human voice.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.080
Of course, utter nonsense.
It's completely impossible.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.180
I've gotten one legal threat from New York.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.360
We're in Sweden, you can't sue me(!)

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.100
Kultur Hotellet, that runs this gallery space,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.360
are generously allowing us to be here
for three or four months.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.760
This space isn't permanent,

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:10.620
but the Museum is permanent
in downtown Helsingborg.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.100
I'd say about a third of the stuff
I've bought on eBay,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.440
the other third I've gotten on specialty forums.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.800
Some of them have been donated,
which is great.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.460
Some of them just come by my office, and say
"I want to give you this", you know?

00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:25.500
One of them was a Palm Pilot:
I took it,

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:28.780
but I can't put it on display
because the Palm Pilot was a success.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.080
Two of these items I've bought
in dark alleys paying cash only.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.760
Can't tell you which ones they are(!)

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.640
All of which leads to one
fairly obvious question:

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.220
What happens if the Museum of Failure...

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.080
fails?

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:46.140
One serious concern that I do have is:

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.360
when a product is overhyped,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.320
and then when it's launched,
it doesn't meet those expectations,

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:52.580
then there's a big risk of failure.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:57.680
So I'd be lying if I said I wasn't nervous
that the Museum of Failure would flop!

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.780
It would have to be on display
at its own museum, so...

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.480
The Museum of Failure is like one of these
perpetual, everlasting machines.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:07.480
It can never fail(!)

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:11.100
Thank you very much to Samuel West
and all the team at the Museum of Failure.

